// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Knowing how the test works is free points. Let's learn the format before we learn the math!","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Digital SAT Math section has two modules of 22 questions each (44 total), with 35 minutes per module. You can use the built-in Desmos graphing calculator on every question."},{"t":"key","text":"The test is adaptive: how you do on Module 1 decides whether Module 2 is easier or harder. Doing well on Module 1 unlocks the higher score range."},{"t":"list","items":["About 75% of questions are multiple choice with 4 options; about 25% are student-produced responses (you type the answer).","There is no penalty for wrong answers, so never leave a question blank.","A reference sheet with common formulas (area, volume, special right triangles) is provided."]},{"t":"p","text":"Scores range from 200 to 800 for Math. The four domains are Algebra, Advanced Math, Problem-Solving and Data Analysis, and Geometry and Trigonometry."}]},{"say":"Smart strategy can raise your score as much as more math. Here are the habits of top scorers.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"With 35 minutes for 22 questions, you have about 1 minute 35 seconds per question."},{"t":"key","text":"Don't get stuck. Flag hard questions with the \"Mark for Review\" tool, guess, move on, and come back if you have time."},{"t":"p","text":"Proven strategies:"},{"t":"list","items":["Plug in the answers: for \"which value of x\" questions, test the choices.","Pick numbers: replace variables with simple numbers to test expressions.","Use Desmos: graph equations to find intersections, zeros and maximums instead of solving by hand.","Read the last line first: know exactly what the question asks (x, or 2x + 1?).","Estimate and eliminate impossible choices."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"If 3(x + 2) = 2x + 11, what is the value of x + 4?","steps":["Solve: 3x + 6 = 2x + 11, so x = 5","Answer the actual question: x + 4"],"answer":"9 (not 5!)"},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Pacing a module","aim":"Each SAT Math module gives you 35 minutes for 22 questions. Plan your pace.","sets":[{"name":"k","start":11,"label":"Questions answered","min":0,"max":22,"step":1},{"name":"s","start":85,"label":"Seconds per question","min":30,"max":150,"step":5}],"outs":[{"name":"used","expr":"k*s/60","label":"Minutes used"},{"name":"left","expr":"35 - k*s/60","label":"Minutes left"},{"name":"perq","expr":"if(k < 22, (35 - k*s/60)/(22 - k), 0)","label":"Minutes left per remaining question"}],"steps":["What pace leaves 5 minutes to check your work at the end?"],"noise":0,"result":"35 minutes ÷ 22 questions is about 95 seconds each. Moving at about 80 seconds on easier questions banks time for the harder ones near the end of the module.","plot":{"x":"k","y":"left"}}]},{"say":"Desmos is the most powerful tool on the Digital SAT, and it's built right into the test. Let's learn to drive it fast.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Desmos graphing calculator is built into Bluebook for every Math question in both modules. Anything you type on a line in the expression list is graphed or calculated instantly."},{"t":"h","text":"The screen"},{"t":"list","items":["The expression list on the left: each line is one equation, function or calculation.","The graph on the right: drag to move, pinch or use + and − to zoom, and the home button to reset the view.","Click a curve to show gray points for its intercepts, maximums, minimums and intersections. Click a gray point to see its exact coordinates."]},{"t":"h","text":"Typing math"},{"t":"list","items":["Exponents: ^ (x^2). Square roots: type sqrt. Absolute value: | | or abs. Pi: type pi.","Fractions: / makes a stacked fraction. Multiplication: * or just a space (2x works).","Arithmetic works on any line: type 0.15*80 and the answer appears.","Graph with x and y. If a question uses another letter, like t or n, rewrite it with x."]},{"t":"desmos","lines":["y = x^2 - 4x + 3","y = sqrt(x + 5)","0.15*80"]},{"t":"tip","text":"Trig questions use degrees, but a calculator may be set to radians. Open the settings (the wrench icon) and check the angle mode before you calculate sin, cos or tan."},{"t":"oops","text":"Desmos shows decimals. If the answer choices are fractions, convert yours (or compare decimals) before you choose."}]},{"say":"Most SAT equations can be solved by graphing. Let's turn algebra into \"find where the graphs meet.\"","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"To solve an equation, graph each side as its own line, y = left side and y = right side, then click the intersection. Its x-coordinate is the solution."},{"t":"p","text":"The intersection method works for any equation, even ones that are hard to solve by hand (radicals, absolute values, exponentials)."},{"t":"p","text":"A second method: graph y = (left side) − (right side) and find where it crosses the x-axis."},{"t":"h","text":"Systems of equations"},{"t":"p","text":"Type both equations exactly as given (Desmos accepts forms like 3x + 2y = 12). The intersection point (x, y) is the solution."},{"t":"list","items":["Lines cross once: one solution.","Parallel lines: no solution.","The same line: infinitely many solutions."]},{"t":"h","text":"Inequalities"},{"t":"p","text":"Type inequalities directly, such as y > 2x − 1. Desmos shades the solution region; for a system, the solution is where the shadings overlap. A point is a solution if it lies in that overlap (on a dashed boundary line doesn't count)."},{"t":"desmos","lines":["y = 3(x + 2)","y = 2x + 11"]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Solve 3(x + 2) = 2x + 11 with Desmos, then find x + 4.","steps":["Type y = 3(x + 2) and y = 2x + 11","Click the intersection: (5, 21), so x = 5"],"answer":"x + 4 = 9 (always answer the question that's asked)"},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Graph both sides","aim":"Desmos solves 3(x + 2) = 2x + 11 by graphing y = left − right and finding where it equals zero. Move x to search.","sets":[{"name":"x","start":1,"label":"x","min":-5,"max":10,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"L","expr":"3*(x + 2)","label":"Left side"},{"name":"Rr","expr":"2*x + 11","label":"Right side"},{"name":"d","expr":"L - Rr","label":"Left − right"}],"steps":["Find the x where left − right equals 0. That's the solution."],"noise":0,"result":"The difference crosses zero at x = 5, the only solution. In Desmos you'd simply click that zero (or the intersection of the two sides), which takes seconds and avoids sign mistakes.","plot":{"x":"x","y":"d"}}]},{"say":"Quadratics and functions are where Desmos saves the most time, and sliders crack the hardest questions.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Graph a quadratic and click it: Desmos shows the vertex (maximum or minimum), the x-intercepts (zeros) and the y-intercept. No factoring or quadratic formula needed."},{"t":"h","text":"Functions"},{"t":"p","text":"Define a function on one line, like f(x) = 2x^2 − 3x, then type f(4) on another line to evaluate it. You can also graph f(x − 2) + 1 to see transformations."},{"t":"h","text":"Sliders for unknown constants"},{"t":"p","text":"When a question has an unknown constant (k, a, c…), type it into the equation. Desmos offers to \"add slider.\" Drag the slider until the graph does what the question describes, such as touching the x-axis once or becoming parallel to another line."},{"t":"desmos","lines":["f(x) = x^2 - 6x + 5","f(4)","y = x^2 - 6x + c"]},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Slide to find c","aim":"For what value of c does x² − 6x + c = 0 have exactly one real solution? Slide c like a Desmos slider.","sets":[{"name":"c","start":5,"label":"c","min":0,"max":15,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"D","expr":"36 - 4*c","label":"Discriminant b² − 4ac"},{"name":"n","expr":"(D > 1e-9)*2 + (abs(D) < 1e-9)*1","label":"Number of real solutions"},{"name":"vy","expr":"c - 9","label":"Vertex y-coordinate"}],"steps":["Watch where the vertex sits as you slide. When does the parabola just touch the x-axis?"],"noise":0,"result":"Exactly one solution happens at c = 9, when the vertex sits on the x-axis (the discriminant is 0). Below 9 there are two solutions, above 9 none. In Desmos, slide c until the vertex touches the axis.","plot":{"x":"c","y":"n"}}]},{"say":"Data questions get faster with lists and tables. Desmos can compute statistics and lines of best fit in seconds.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Type a list in square brackets, then use statistics functions on it: mean, median, stdev and more. For a model, enter data in a table and use the ~ symbol to fit a regression."},{"t":"h","text":"Lists and statistics"},{"t":"list","items":["Name a list: L = [12, 15, 15, 18, 40]","mean(L), median(L), stdev(L) (sample standard deviation), total(L) (the sum) and length(L) (how many values)","Change one value to see how the mean and median react to an outlier."]},{"t":"h","text":"Tables and regression"},{"t":"p","text":"Add a table (the + button), enter x₁ and y₁ values, then type y₁ ~ mx₁ + b for a linear fit. Desmos reports m, b and r². Use y₁ ~ ax₁^2 + bx₁ + c for a quadratic fit or y₁ ~ ab^x₁ for an exponential fit."},{"t":"desmos","lines":["L = [12, 15, 15, 18, 40]","mean(L)","median(L)","y_1 ~ m x_1 + b"]},{"t":"tip","text":"You rarely need a regression on the SAT, but lists are great for mean and median questions: typing beats adding by hand."}]},{"say":"Desmos is fast, but only if you know when to reach for it. Let's build the judgment top scorers have.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Use Desmos when a graph answers the question: intersections, zeros, maximums, number of solutions, inequalities and checking answers. Use quick algebra or mental math when the problem is a one-step calculation or the question is about structure."},{"t":"h","text":"Great Desmos moves"},{"t":"list","items":["Solve any equation or system by graphing.","Check equivalent expressions: graph y = (expression) and each answer choice. The one whose graph lies exactly on top is equivalent.","Check a multiple-choice answer by plugging it in on a new line.","Find how many solutions an equation has by counting intersections."]},{"t":"h","text":"When to skip it"},{"t":"list","items":["One-step arithmetic you can do in your head.","Questions about meaning, like \"what does the 15 represent in this model?\"","Very large or tiny numbers that are hard to see on the graph (calculate instead)."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Which expression is equivalent to (x + 3)² − 9? Choices: x² + 6x, x² + 9, x² − 6x, x² + 6x + 9.","steps":["Graph y = (x + 3)^2 − 9","Graph each choice; only y = x^2 + 6x lands exactly on top"],"answer":"x² + 6x"},{"t":"oops","text":"Watch for answers that need a specific form. Desmos finds x = 0.4, but the choices may show 2/5."}]},{"say":"About a quarter of SAT Math questions have no answer choices. You type the answer, and small entry mistakes cost real points.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"For student-produced response questions, you type your answer into a box. Follow the entry rules exactly; Bluebook shows a preview of how your answer will be read."},{"t":"h","text":"The entry rules"},{"t":"list","items":["You can enter up to 5 characters for a positive answer and up to 6 (including the minus sign) for a negative answer.","Fractions and decimals are both fine: 2/3, .6666 and .6667 are all accepted.","If a decimal is too long, round or cut it off so it fills all the space, but don't shorten it more than that (0.67 would be marked wrong for 2/3).","Write mixed numbers as improper fractions or decimals: 3½ is 7/2 or 3.5, never 3 1/2.","Don't type symbols such as %, $ or commas.","If more than one answer is correct, enter just one."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Your answer is 5/12. How could you enter it?","steps":["5/12 fits in 4 characters","As a decimal, 0.41666… → .4166 or .4167"],"answer":"5/12, .4166 or .4167"},{"t":"tip","text":"When Desmos gives a long decimal like 1.33333, enter the exact fraction (4/3) instead. It's shorter and always accepted."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Rounding to fill the box","aim":"An answer of 2/3 = 0.6666… is entered with d decimal places. Fewer places means a bigger error. The SAT accepts .6666 or .6667 (4 decimal places).","sets":[{"name":"d","start":2,"label":"Decimal places entered","min":1,"max":4,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"v","expr":"round(2/3*10^d)/10^d","label":"Rounded entry"},{"name":"err","expr":"abs(v - 2/3)","label":"Error from 2/3"}],"steps":["Which number of decimal places is enough to be accepted?"],"noise":0,"result":"Rounding to fill the box (4 decimal places for a positive number under 1) keeps the error tiny. Shorter entries like 0.67 lose accuracy and are marked wrong.","plot":{"x":"d","y":"err"}}]},{"say":"The last few points come from preparation outside the math: the reference sheet, practice tests and a calm test day.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Bluebook gives you a reference sheet with area and volume formulas, the Pythagorean theorem and special right triangles. Know what's on it so you only open it when needed, and memorize what's not (like slope, the quadratic formula and circle equations)."},{"t":"h","text":"Before the test"},{"t":"list","items":["Take the full-length practice tests in the Bluebook app. They use the same adaptive format, tools and Desmos as the real test.","Practice with Desmos until the common moves (intersections, vertex, sliders) take seconds."]},{"t":"h","text":"Test day"},{"t":"list","items":["Bring your charged device with Bluebook installed, your admission ticket and an acceptable photo ID, plus a pencil or pen. Scratch paper is provided.","Reading and Writing comes first (2 modules), then a 10-minute break, then Math (2 modules), about 2 hours 14 minutes in total.","Within a module you can move back and forth and use Mark for Review, but you can't return to a module once it ends."]},{"t":"h","text":"Scores"},{"t":"p","text":"The total score is 400 to 1600. Many colleges superscore: they take your best section scores across test dates."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Superscore calculator","aim":"You took the SAT twice. A college that superscores combines your best Reading and Writing and best Math scores.","sets":[{"name":"rw1","start":620,"label":"Test 1 Reading and Writing","min":200,"max":800,"step":10},{"name":"m1","start":580,"label":"Test 1 Math","min":200,"max":800,"step":10},{"name":"rw2","start":590,"label":"Test 2 Reading and Writing","min":200,"max":800,"step":10},{"name":"m2","start":650,"label":"Test 2 Math","min":200,"max":800,"step":10}],"outs":[{"name":"t1","expr":"rw1 + m1","label":"Test 1 total"},{"name":"t2","expr":"rw2 + m2","label":"Test 2 total"},{"name":"sup","expr":"max(rw1, rw2) + max(m1, m2)","label":"Superscore"}],"steps":["Compare the superscore with each single test total."],"noise":0,"result":"The superscore (here 620 + 650 = 1270) can beat either single test. Check each college's policy: some superscore, some want all scores, and Score Choice lets you pick which dates to send to many schools.","plot":{"x":"m2","y":"sup"}}]}],[{"say":"Algebra is the biggest chunk of SAT Math. Linear equations are the foundation, so let's make them automatic.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Solve linear equations by distributing, combining like terms, and isolating the variable."},{"t":"p","text":"Special cases the SAT loves:"},{"t":"list","items":["No solution: variables cancel, leaving a false statement (3 = 5). Happens when the x-coefficients match but constants differ.","Infinitely many solutions: both sides are identical."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"For what value of k does 4x + 7 = kx − 2 have no solution?","steps":["No solution when the x-terms cancel but the constants differ","k must equal 4 (then 7 = −2 is false)"],"answer":"k = 4"},{"t":"tip","text":"For equations with fractions, multiply everything by the common denominator first."}]},{"say":"The SAT tests linear functions in equations, graphs, tables and real-world stories. Know how to move between them.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"In y = mx + b, m is the slope (rate of change) and b is the y-intercept (starting value)."},{"t":"p","text":"In word problems, the slope is the \"per\" amount and the intercept is the starting amount."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A plumber charges C = 75h + 60, where h is hours. What does 60 represent?","steps":["When h = 0, C = 60"],"answer":"A flat fee of $60 charged even before any work is done"},{"t":"p","text":"Parallel lines have equal slopes; perpendicular slopes multiply to −1. To find the slope from two points, use (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁)."},{"t":"tip","text":"For questions about a line's equation from a table, find the slope from two rows, then plug in one point to find b."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Temperature conversion line","aim":"Fahrenheit and Celsius are related by the linear function F = 1.8C + 32.","sets":[{"name":"C","start":20,"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"F","expr":"1.8*C + 32","label":"Temperature (°F)"}],"steps":["Interpret the slope and the y-intercept. Where are the two scales equal?"],"noise":0,"result":"The slope 1.8 means each 1 °C rise is a 1.8 °F rise; the intercept 32 is the freezing point. Solving C = 1.8C + 32 gives −40, where both scales read the same.","plot":{"x":"C","y":"F"}}]},{"say":"Systems of equations show up in nearly every SAT. Learn the fastest method for each type.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Solve systems by substitution, elimination, or graphing in Desmos (find the intersection)."},{"t":"key","text":"A system of two linear equations has no solution if the lines are parallel (same slope, different intercepts) and infinitely many if they're the same line."},{"t":"ex","problem":"For what value of a does the system 3x + 2y = 5 and ax + 4y = 9 have no solution?","steps":["No solution: parallel lines with equal slopes","Multiply the first by 2: 6x + 4y = 10; matching x-coefficient means a = 6","6x + 4y = 9 vs. 10: different constants, so parallel"],"answer":"a = 6"},{"t":"p","text":"For inequalities, solve like equations but flip the sign when multiplying or dividing by a negative. On a graph, test a point to decide which side to shade."}]}],[{"say":"Quadratics are the heart of Advanced Math. You need to know their forms and what each one tells you.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Standard form y = ax² + bx + c: c is the y-intercept.","Factored form y = a(x − p)(x − q): zeros at x = p and x = q.","Vertex form y = a(x − h)² + k: vertex at (h, k)."]},{"t":"key","text":"The vertex's x-coordinate is −b/(2a), halfway between the zeros. The sum of the solutions is −b/a and the product is c/a."},{"t":"ex","problem":"What is the sum of the solutions of 2x² − 10x + 7 = 0?","steps":["Sum of roots = −b/a","−(−10)/2"],"answer":"5"},{"t":"p","text":"The discriminant b² − 4ac tells you the number of real solutions: positive → 2, zero → 1, negative → 0. Desmos can find zeros and vertices instantly."}]},{"say":"Exponential growth and decay show up in finance, populations and science questions. Learn to read them fast.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"f(x) = a(b)ˣ: a is the starting value, and b is the growth factor. b > 1 means growth; 0 < b < 1 means decay."},{"t":"list","items":["Growth by r%: b = 1 + r/100. A 7% increase gives b = 1.07.","Decay by r%: b = 1 − r/100. A 15% decrease gives b = 0.85.","If the time unit changes, adjust the exponent: doubling every 3 years is a(2)^(t/3)."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"A car worth $24,000 loses 12% of its value each year. Write its value after t years.","steps":["Decay factor: 1 − 0.12 = 0.88"],"answer":"V = 24,000(0.88)ᵗ"},{"t":"p","text":"Linear functions change by a constant amount; exponential functions change by a constant percent (factor)."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"A growing town","aim":"A town of 12,000 people grows by a fixed percentage each year. Model it as P = 12,000(1 + r)ᵗ.","sets":[{"name":"t","start":10,"label":"Years","min":0,"max":40,"step":1},{"name":"r","start":3,"label":"Growth rate (% per year)","min":0.5,"max":8,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"P","expr":"12000*(1 + r/100)^t","label":"Population"}],"steps":["Compare linear growth (adding 360 people a year) with 3% growth after 40 years."],"noise":0,"result":"Exponential growth multiplies by the same factor (1.03) each year, so it eventually beats any linear growth. SAT questions often ask you to read the initial value and growth factor from the equation.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"P"}}]},{"say":"Advanced Math also tests your fluency with expressions and functions. These skills save tons of time.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Exponent rules: xᵃ · xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ, x^(1/n) = ⁿ√x.","Factor and simplify rational expressions; watch for values that make denominators zero.","Remainder Theorem: p(a) is the remainder when p(x) is divided by (x − a)."]},{"t":"key","text":"Function notation: f(3) means plug in 3. f(x + 1) means replace every x with (x + 1). g(f(2)) means find f(2), then plug that into g."},{"t":"p","text":"Transformations: f(x) + k shifts up k; f(x − h) shifts right h; −f(x) reflects over the x-axis."},{"t":"ex","problem":"f(x) = x² − 3. What is f(a + 1)?","steps":["Replace x with (a + 1): (a + 1)² − 3"],"answer":"a² + 2a − 2"}]}],[{"say":"These real-world math skills are some of the most reliable points on the SAT. Let's master them.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Ratios and proportions: set up a/b = c/d and cross-multiply.","Unit conversion: multiply by conversion factors so units cancel.","Percent of: part = percent × whole."]},{"t":"key","text":"Percent change = (new − old) ÷ old × 100%. Two successive changes multiply: a 20% increase then a 20% decrease gives 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96, a 4% overall decrease."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A $80 jacket is marked up 25%, then discounted 10%. Final price?","steps":["80 × 1.25 = 100","100 × 0.90"],"answer":"$90"},{"t":"oops","text":"A 50% increase followed by a 50% decrease does NOT return to the original. 100 → 150 → 75."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Cost of a road trip","aim":"Plan the fuel cost of a 300-mile trip from your car's gas mileage and the price of gas.","sets":[{"name":"mpg","start":30,"label":"Gas mileage (miles per gallon)","min":10,"max":60,"step":1},{"name":"p","start":3.5,"label":"Gas price ($ per gallon)","min":2,"max":6,"step":0.1}],"outs":[{"name":"gal","expr":"300/mpg","label":"Gallons used"},{"name":"cost","expr":"gal*p","label":"Fuel cost ($)"}],"steps":["Set up the units: miles ÷ (miles/gallon) × (dollars/gallon) = dollars."],"noise":0,"result":"Units cancel to give dollars: 300 ÷ 30 × $3.50 = $35. Cost is inversely proportional to gas mileage, so improving from 20 to 40 mpg halves the cost.","plot":{"x":"mpg","y":"cost"}}]},{"say":"Data questions ask you to read graphs and reason about center and spread. Careful reading wins these.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Mean (average), median (middle value), mode (most common), range (max − min).","Standard deviation measures spread: more spread out data has a larger standard deviation."]},{"t":"key","text":"Outliers pull the mean toward them but barely affect the median. Adding an extreme value changes the mean and standard deviation the most."},{"t":"p","text":"Scatterplots and lines of best fit: the slope of the line is the predicted change in y per unit x. Use the line to predict, but be cautious extrapolating far outside the data."},{"t":"p","text":"Sampling: results can be generalized only to the population that was randomly sampled. A random sample avoids bias; larger samples give smaller margins of error. Only randomly assigned experiments can show cause and effect."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A survey of 200 randomly chosen students at one school finds 60% prefer later start times. To whom can this be generalized?","steps":["The sample was random from that school"],"answer":"All students at that school (not all students nationwide)"}]},{"say":"Probability questions on the SAT usually come with a table. The key is reading the right row or column.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Probability = favorable outcomes ÷ total outcomes. In a two-way table, the \"given\" condition tells you which row or column is your total."},{"t":"ex","problem":"In a survey, 40 of 100 students play a sport, and 15 of those 40 also play an instrument. If a sport player is chosen at random, what is the probability they play an instrument?","steps":["\"Given they play a sport\" means the total is 40, not 100","15/40"],"answer":"0.375"},{"t":"list","items":["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Expected value = sum of each outcome × its probability."]},{"t":"tip","text":"Underline the words \"given,\" \"of those\" or \"among\" in the question. They tell you the denominator."}]}],[{"say":"Geometry is a smaller part of the SAT, but the reference sheet and a few key facts make it very doable.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The reference sheet gives formulas for area (rectangle, triangle, circle), volume (prism, cylinder, sphere, cone, pyramid), and special right triangles. Know how to use them, especially when a question gives volume and asks for a dimension."},{"t":"key","text":"Angles: a straight line is 180°, a triangle's angles sum to 180°, and vertical angles are equal. Parallel lines cut by a transversal make equal corresponding and alternate interior angles."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A cylinder has volume 72π and radius 3. Find its height.","steps":["V = πr²h, so 72π = π(9)h"],"answer":"h = 8"},{"t":"p","text":"Similar triangles have proportional sides. If two triangles share angles (AA), set up a proportion."}]},{"say":"Right triangle questions combine the Pythagorean theorem, special triangles and SOH-CAH-TOA.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Pythagorean theorem: a² + b² = c². Know common triples: 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17 (and multiples)."},{"t":"p","text":"Special right triangles: 45-45-90 sides x, x, x√2; 30-60-90 sides x, x√3, 2x."},{"t":"key","text":"SOH-CAH-TOA, and the SAT favorite: sin(x) = cos(90° − x). Complementary angles swap sine and cosine."},{"t":"ex","problem":"In a right triangle, sin A = 5/13. What is cos B, where B is the other acute angle?","steps":["A and B are complementary","cos B = sin A"],"answer":"5/13"},{"t":"p","text":"Radians: 180° = π radians. Multiply degrees by π/180 to convert."}]},{"say":"Circle questions test equations, arcs and angles. Completing the square is the big skill here.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Circumference C = 2πr; area A = πr². An arc's length or a sector's area is a fraction (angle ÷ 360°) of the whole."},{"t":"key","text":"The equation of a circle is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², with center (h, k) and radius r."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the center and radius of x² + y² − 6x + 4y = 12","steps":["Group and complete the square: (x² − 6x + 9) + (y² + 4y + 4) = 12 + 9 + 4","(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25"],"answer":"center (3, −2), radius 5"},{"t":"p","text":"A tangent line is perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency. A central angle equals its arc; an inscribed angle is half its arc."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Will the truck fit through the tunnel?","aim":"A tunnel's cross-section is a semicircle. How tall is the opening at a distance x from the center?","sets":[{"name":"x","start":3,"label":"Distance from the center (m)","min":0,"max":5,"step":0.1},{"name":"r","start":6,"label":"Tunnel radius (m)","min":5,"max":8,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"y","expr":"sqrt(r^2 - x^2)","label":"Height of the tunnel there (m)"}],"steps":["A truck is 4 m wide and 4.5 m tall, driving in the middle. Check the height at x = 2."],"noise":0,"result":"The circle x² + y² = r² gives y = √(r² − x²). With r = 6, at x = 2 the clearance is 5.66 m, so the 4.5 m truck fits; near the walls the height drops quickly.","plot":{"x":"x","y":"y"}}]}]];
export default text;
